  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. resource_efficiency (denominator weighting)
       Q: How should energy consumption, raw-material inputs, and machine-utilisation time be combined into a single 'resource consumed' denominator — for example, are they weighted by their dollar cost, or by some other normalisation coefficient? Without explicit weights the optimiser will treat 1 kWh, 1 kg of material, and 1 machine-hour as equivalent, which could distort the efficiency ratio by up to 10× depending on actual cost ratios.
       A: Use monetary cost as the common denominator: aggregate resources by their standard cost rates (e.g. $/kWh, $/kg, $/machine-hour) as recorded in the facility ERP; if cost data are unavailable, weight energy:material:machine-time equally at 1/3 each.
    2. resource_efficiency (minimum output floor)
       Q: Is there a minimum total output value or unit-count that must be delivered during the shift, regardless of how high the efficiency ratio is? Without a floor, the optimiser could maximise the ratio by running only a single high-value, low-resource job and idling for the rest of the shift.
       A: Apply a minimum throughput floor equal to 80% of the shift's standard production target (or the most recent historical average if no formal target exists); treat this as a soft lower-bound penalty rather than a hard infeasibility.
    3. operator_fatigue (measurement timing)
       Q: Should the fatigue ≤ 0.4 hard constraint be satisfied at every point in time throughout the shift (continuous enforcement), or only at the end of the shift (final-state enforcement)? A mid-shift intensity spike can push fatigue above 0.4 transiently even if the final reading is within limits.
       A: Enforce the constraint continuously: the fatigue level must remain ≤ 0.4 at every scheduling interval (e.g. every 15 minutes); brief exceedances are treated as constraint violations even if the shift-end value recovers below 0.4.
    4. priority_order vs. _raw_priority_order (conflict)
       Q: The formal priority order lists only 'HumanWellbeing', implying operator fatigue and noise always dominate resource efficiency — but the raw intent order lists resource_efficiency first. If reducing cycle rate by 20% would lower fatigue from 0.38 to 0.22 but cut efficiency by 15%, should the optimiser accept that trade or resist it because efficiency is the primary business objective?
       A: Treat HumanWellbeing (fatigue and noise hard constraints) as lexicographically first: no efficiency gain may push fatigue above its 0.4 limit or noise above 80 dB; within the feasible region, maximise resource_efficiency without further fatigue minimisation beyond constraint satisfaction.
    5. noise_level (averaging window)
       Q: Is the 80 dB limit a full-shift time-weighted equivalent level (LAeq over the entire shift duration, e.g. 8 h), or must every shorter measurement interval (e.g. per-minute LAeq) also stay at or below 80 dB? Brief machining bursts may spike above 80 dB even when the shift average is compliant.
       A: Apply the constraint as a per-15-minute LAeq ≤ 80 dB, consistent with EU Directive 2003/10/EC action-value monitoring practice; this is stricter than a shift-average interpretation and provides a safety margin against sustained operator exposure hotspots.

