  [Escalation] Conflict summary:
    A proposed schedule triggered by a 20% demand surge has been rejected by the CBPA constraint engine due to simultaneous violations of two human-centric safety constraints. The FatigueIndex reached 0.6001 against a hard ceiling of 0.40 (excess margin: +0.2001, representing a 50% overshoot), and workplace Noise reached 84.9 dB against a ceiling of 80.0 dB (excess margin: +4.9 dB). Both violations occurred because the solver attempted to compress production into existing shift windows to absorb the full demand increase without adding capacity. Human-welfare constraints cannot be silently relaxed by the scheduler; escalation to the production manager is mandatory before any revised schedule can be committed. The ranked options below progress from full constraint preservation (with a delivery impact) to targeted, audited constraint relaxation (with reduced delivery impact but increased compliance burden).

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Redistribute load across additional shifts (preferred): Accept only the portion of the 20% demand increase that can be absorbed within current FatigueIndex and Noise limits by spreading production across one or two additional partial shifts per week. The unfulfilled demand (~15–20%) is deferred to the following production cycle or negotiated with the customer as a phased delivery. No safety limits are changed. This is the lowest-risk path and requires no regulatory disclosure.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Relax Noise ceiling to 85.0 dB with mandatory PPE protocol: Temporarily raise the Noise constraint to 85.0 dB (the OSHA permissible exposure limit for an 8-hour shift), contingent on issuing and logging mandatory hearing-protection equipment for all affected workers and limiting continuous high-noise exposure windows to 4 hours maximum per shift. Fatigue must be separately managed by capping overtime to reduce FatigueIndex to ≤ 0.40, which will still require partial deferral of demand. This option resolves the Noise violation while keeping Fatigue within bounds, achieving a reduced but non-zero deadline gap. Requires EHS sign-off and documented worker acknowledgement.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 — Relax FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.55 with mandatory rotation and rest protocol: Temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.55 (a 37.5% increase over the baseline limit) for a defined period not to exceed two consecutive weeks, contingent on implementing a mandatory job-rotation schedule every 90 minutes, inserting a minimum 15-minute ergonomic rest break per 3-hour block, and assigning a safety observer to each affected line. This option, combined with the Noise relaxation in Option 2, would allow near-full demand fulfillment but carries the highest compliance and worker-welfare risk. Requires written manager authorization, union notification if applicable, and post-period fatigue audit. Use only if the customer delivery penalty outweighs the combined compliance and health risk.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
