  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. value_per_resource_consumed / resource_aggregation_method
       Q: How should heterogeneous resources (labor-hours, kWh, raw-material kg, machine-hours) be combined into a single denominator — by monetary cost-weight, by a predefined physical index, or should only one resource type appear in the denominator? Without this, the optimizer has no unique objective surface to maximize.
       A: Aggregate all consumed resources by their standard monetary unit cost from the last accounting period; if cost data are unavailable, use total energy consumed (kWh) as the sole denominator and weight labor at the fully-loaded hourly rate converted to kWh-equivalent.
    2. priority_order vs context._raw_priority_order (structural conflict)
       Q: The formal priority stack lists only 'HumanWellbeing', but the raw priority order places value_per_resource_consumed first — should efficiency maximization be strictly subordinate to the fatigue and noise envelope (lexicographic), or are marginal wellbeing trade-offs against efficiency gains ever permissible (weighted)?
       A: Treat HumanWellbeing as lexicographically dominant: maximize efficiency only within schedules that never violate fatigue ≤ 0.4 or noise ≤ 80 dB; no efficiency gain justifies relaxing either hard limit.
    3. operator_fatigue_index / cumulative measurement epoch
       Q: Does the 0.4 fatigue ceiling apply at every scheduling epoch throughout the shift (instantaneous hard ceiling), or only to the cumulative index at shift-end — because a schedule that peaks at 0.55 mid-shift but recovers to 0.38 by the end would pass the end-point check but violate ergonomic intent?
       A: Enforce the ≤ 0.4 limit as a hard ceiling at every scheduling epoch (e.g., each 15-minute slot), not just at shift-end; any schedule that breaches 0.4 at any point during the shift is rejected.
    4. value_per_resource_consumed / value_definition (throughput vs. revenue vs. margin)
       Q: Should 'value' in the efficiency ratio be physical throughput units (all variants equal), revenue-equivalent, or contribution margin — and if the product mix varies this shift, which variant's value rate governs? Throughput-optimal and margin-optimal schedules can diverge by 20–30% in mixed-model lines.
       A: Use contribution margin (selling price minus variable material cost per unit) as the value metric; if live pricing data are unavailable, fall back to physical throughput with all variants weighted equally and flag the assumption for post-shift review.
    5. ambient_noise_level / feasibility for inherently loud high-value operations
       Q: If certain high-value operations (e.g., heavy pressing, cutting) have a minimum achievable noise floor above 80 dB that cannot be reduced by scheduling or sequencing, should the optimizer exclude those operations entirely, schedule them only with PPE-credited noise reduction, or trigger an infeasibility alert for manager override?
       A: Exclude any operation whose minimum achievable time-weighted average noise level exceeds 80 dB from the feasible schedule; do not credit PPE use toward the acoustic hard constraint, and surface an infeasibility warning if exclusion eliminates all high-value work orders.

